<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hrvoje Vukčić Hrvatinić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1350–<a href="/years/1416">1416</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hrvoje Vukčić Hrvatinić was the most powerful Bosnian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> of his day, who made and unmade Bosnian kings after the death of Tvrtko I. Backing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a> against Sigismund for the Hungarian crown, he received the title Duke of Split and the central Dalmatian islands in 1403. His missal, the Hrvoje Missal, is a celebrated Glagolitic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hrvoje-vukcic-hrvatinic.jpg" alt="Hrvoje Vukčić Hrvatinić" width="237" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: scribe Butko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hrvoje_Vukcic_Hrvatinic_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hrvoje was the eldest son of Duke Vukac Hrvatinić and the most prominent member of the Hrvatinić noble family. His brothers were Vuk, who became Ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, Dragiša and Vojislav, and his sister Resa Vukčić married Batalo Šantić. He is first mentioned in 1376 as a prince and knight under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a>. His lands were the Donji Kraji, the Lower Edges of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, facing Croatia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> to the west. He married Jelena Nelipčić, a granddaughter of the Croatian noble Ivan I Nelipac.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>King Tvrtko I made him Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> in 1380, with a seat in Lašva, and in 1387 he led Bosnian troops into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> to raise the siege of Bishop Ivan Horvat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>. In the succession struggle that followed the death of Louis I he sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Sigismund of Luxembourg</a>. He fought the Ottoman Turks in Bosnia in 1392 under King Stephen Dabiša, opposed Queen Jelena Gruba, and took part in the choice of Stephen Ostoja as king in May 1398.</p><p>When Sigismund invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, Hrvoje defeated his forces before they reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vrbas">Vrbas</a>, pursued them across the river Una and took the župa of Dubica. By the end of 1402 he had brought every Dalmatian city except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> to recognise Ladislaus, who after his coronation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a> named him Vicar General for Sclavonia. Coins Hrvoje minted in Split styled him duke of Split, royal viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and supreme voivode of Bosnia. In 1406 he strengthened Prozor Fortress above the Vrlika valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hrvoje later fell out with Ostoja and joined the plot that replaced him with Tvrtko II Kotromanić in 1404. After Sigismund's intervention of 1408 and the massacre of the Bosnian army he came to terms with Sigismund, but Ostoja's return weakened him and he lost Split and the islands. He then turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. The Hungarian army was defeated at Lašva in 1415, which opened the way to Ottoman expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>. Hrvoje died the next year and was buried in the crypt of his underground chapel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a>; his widow married Ostoja. His missal, written in Glagolitic, is now in the Topkapı Palace Museum Manuscript Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hrvoje Vukčić Hrvatinić” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the struggle for the Hungarian crown between Sigismund of Luxembourg and Ladislaus of Naples, the powerful Bosnian noble <b>Hrvoje Vukčić Hrvatinić</b> backed Ladislaus.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-split">Duchy of Split</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal power then weakened as great nobles such as <b>Hrvoje Vukčić Hrvatinić</b> and Stjepan Vukčić Kosača, whose lands became Herzegovina, made and unmade kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bosnia">Kingdom of Bosnia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kotor Varoš, in what is now <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.62,17.37&amp;year=1392" aria-label="Kotor Varoš on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hrvoje Vukčić Hrvatinić"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4237 3721h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4237 3721h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.62,17.37&amp;year=1392">What was here in 1392? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
